The limit can also be found directly, without memorizing the three cases, as done here.

This calculation uses the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}h(x)
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{x^2-3}{x^4+9}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{x^2-3}{x^{4}}}{\dfrac{x^4+9}{x^{4}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide both sides by }x^{4}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{\cancel{x^2}}{x^2\cdot\cancel{x^2}}-\dfrac{3}{x^4}}{\dfrac{\cancel{x^4}}{\cancel{x^4}}+\dfrac{9}{x^4}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{x^2}-\dfrac{3}{x^4}}{1+\dfrac{9}{x^4}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{0-0}{1+0}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{0}{1}
\\\\
&=0
\end{align}$
